{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01NmAksYX7xPWBrWMbTtFL9x","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the research data carefully to define three tiers for the Dream simulation.\n\nKey evidence from the data:\n\n1. **IRT γ results**:\n   - Oracle GSM8K: γ=+3.77, significant (CI excludes zero)\n   - Oracle ARC 2PL: γ=-0.618 (CI: -1.04 to -0.2), significant\n   - Oracle ARC 4PL: γ=-1.21 (CI: -2.53 to 0.12), marginally significant\n   - S1-only ARC: γ=-1.46, significant (CI excludes zero)\n   - S1-only GSM8K shows no significant effect (γ=0.19, CI: -0.92 to 1.33), while the RRF-based MMLU approach completely failed to converge\n\n2. **Calibration performance**:\n   - IRT modeling consistently underperforms the raw baseline across most conditions, with MAE values ranging from 0.005 to 0.092, though S1-only ARC is the exception where IRT actually improves calibration. Coverage also degrades substantially, collapsing to 18-33% at the 95% nominal level.\n\n3. **Signal effectiveness**:\n   - S1 embedding similarity is the only reliable signal with AUC~0.98 at 0.5B scale, while both S2 (Min-K++) and S4 (Self-Critique) fail dramatically with AUC~0.47-0.48. Min-K++ even shows a strong directional reversal (Cohen's d = -1.98), and only 1 out of 3 signals proves effective at this scale.\n\n4. **Contamination effects**:\n   - The 0.5B ARC model exhibits catastrophic forgetting with a 17.5 percentage point drop at d100, confirming that negative gamma effects are genuine.\n   - GSM8K shows near-zero contamination effects due to its open-ended nature, while MMLU displays modest inflation of 1-3pp at larger scales but more pronounced effects at 0.5B.\n\n5. **Model complexity**:\n   - Four-parameter logistic curves fit multiple-choice questions best, whereas two-parameter curves work better for open-ended tasks.\n\n6. **Current failures**:\n   - Multiple experiments have collapsed—the simulation sweep failed repeatedly, noise propagation analysis produced negative results, and MMLU IRT fitting didn't work at all, with coverage breaking down across the board, likely because eight models isn't enough data.\n\n7. **Paper framing pivot**:\n   - I'm shifting away from claiming calibration improvements and instead positioning this as a systematic detection and benchmark-dependent characterization framework.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the three outcome scenarios. In the best case, the remaining experiments converge successfully—the sweep works with more seeds, larger datasets, and better signal data, MMLU IRT fits with more models, coverage improves above sixteen models, and gamma significance holds across benchmarks. The calibration improvements might not beat raw performance everywhere, but they'd show meaningful corrections on contaminated data, and the heterogeneous gamma values across benchmarks would strongly support the characterization story. In the median scenario, some experiments work but others don't—MMLU remains problematic, coverage stays poor, and gamma significance only appears on ARC and possibly GSM8K with oracle data, though I could still frame this as a detection and characterization story by emphasizing signal effectiveness and the identifiability analysis.\n\nThe worst case is when calibration fails to work reliably, coverage doesn't improve, gamma only shows significance with oracle exposure rather than actual signals, and the IRT framework becomes more of a liability than an asset. There I'd need to pivot entirely toward characterizing signal effectiveness and presenting the negative results about why IRT calibration is harder than expected.\n\nTo track these outcomes, I'm identifying the specific metrics that matter: how many benchmarks show gamma significance with real signals, the MAE improvement over raw baselines, actual coverage at 95% confidence, how many IRT fits converge, signal AUC performance on the larger datasets, and ranking correlation metrics.\n\nLooking at the current best results across benchmarks, coverage is consistently falling short of the 0.95 target—ranging from 0.18 to 0.625—while MAE results are mixed. I need to project how things scale when I increase the model count to 16-30 through multi-seed runs and test on larger signal data at 1.5B and 3B parameters.\n\nNow I'm laying out the success criteria across three tiers. For the best-case scenario, I need γ to be significant on at least two real benchmarks, see S2/S4 improvements at larger data scales, push coverage above 60% with sufficient models, beat the baseline on contaminated conditions, validate identifiability through simulation, achieve MMLU convergence, and demonstrate clear cross-benchmark patterns with strong seed consistency. The median tier requires γ significance on just one or two benchmarks with oracle data.\n\nFor the worst case, I'm looking at γ only showing up with oracle exposure, no real-signal improvements, coverage staying below 30%, MMLU failing to converge, and having to reframe the whole paper around negative results. I should also reconsider which of the six evaluation metrics—MAE, coverage at different levels, sharpness, Kendall τ, dose-response monotonicity, and SBC—are most critical for defining these tiers.\n\nLooking at the actual numbers, ARC with S1-only shows real calibration gains (MAE drops from 0.059 to 0.035, coverage at 95% hits 0.625), but GSM8K tells a different story where IRT performs worse on MAE despite oracle fitting. The mixed results mean I need to be careful about what constitutes \"best\" versus \"worst\"—ARC validates the approach while GSM8K suggests the method struggles when contamination effects are naturally minimal.\n\nWith a larger model set (16-30 models), I'd expect MMLU to stabilize and show meaningful effects, ARC's calibration advantage to become more pronounced, and GSM8K to correctly identify as needing minimal adjustment. For the metrics, I'm thinking significant gamma estimates on at least two-thirds of benchmarks, with ARC showing negative gamma around -0.5 to -3, MMLU positive around 0.3 to 2.0, and GSM8K near zero—plus MAE improvements on contaminated conditions and coverage in the 50-75% range, though still below the nominal 95%.\n\nFor signal detection, I'd want S1 to maintain AUC above 0.95 across all model scales, with S2 or S4 reaching at least 0.65 on larger models, and Kendall tau correlations above 0.75. The IRT models should converge cleanly with rhat under 1.01 and effective sample sizes above 500. In a more realistic scenario, I might only see gamma significance on one or two benchmarks with ARC confirmed but MMLU marginal, comparable MAE performance, coverage between 25-50%, and tau around 0.60-0.80, with only S1 proving effective. At worst, gamma effects would only appear with oracle signals rather than actual detection methods, and MAE would underperform the baseline across the board.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the narrative framing. The core story is that ContamCal works as a systematic framework revealing how contamination effects vary by benchmark—positive on MMLU, negative on ARC, null on GSM8K—while delivering calibrated scores that beat raw performance where gamma is significant. The signal analysis showing Min-K++ reversals and S1-only effectiveness at smaller scales strengthens this. The three main claims hold up, though the calibration improvement claim becomes more precise: it's specifically on benchmarks where gamma is significant, not universally.\n\nIf the calibration results are weaker, I'd pivot to emphasizing gamma itself as the key insight—it's a measurable property that characterizes benchmark-dependent contamination susceptibility. The signal characterization becomes the strongest empirical contribution, and the Fisher Information bounds provide theoretical grounding. This shifts the framing from \"calibration improves performance\" to \"contamination-aware modeling reveals benchmark properties,\" and I'd lead with the signal analysis and identifiability results instead. The coverage limitations with small N become an honest discussion of IRT's fundamental constraints rather than a weakness.\n\nAt the weakest, if calibration provides no practical benefit, the paper reframes entirely as a negative-results contribution: \"Why Statistical Calibration of Contaminated Benchmarks is Harder Than You Think,\" leading with characterization and theoretical insights rather than practical improvements.\n\nNow I'm formalizing the key metrics into structured data—defining gamma significance across benchmarks, the MAE deltas showing where IRT underperforms, coverage fractions, correlation measures, and effective signal counts.\n\nStructuring the metrics... Looking at the signal strengths across model scales — at 0.5B the convergence is strong but S2 and S4 are weak, which tracks with what I'm seeing in the larger models. For projections with more data, coverage should improve but probably not linearly — going from N=8 to N=16 might push ARC coverage from 0.625 up to around 0.70-0.80, though real data is messier than the simulations suggested. Let me write out the YAML now with these specifics, being clear about what's grounded versus what's extrapolation.\n\nThe framing has shifted from pure calibration improvement to detection and characterization, so I need to structure the scenarios around whether the characterization story holds up and whether we get calibration wins on top of that. The key metrics to track are whether gamma estimates exclude zero on real signals, how much IRT reduces MAE compared to raw predictions, coverage at 95% confidence, ranking correlation via Kendall's tau, whether non-S1 signals recover at larger scales, whether per-benchmark gamma values show interpretable patterns, convergence quality of the IRT fits, and Fisher information metrics.\n\nNow I'm thinking through how to define the expected ranges for each tier. The user wants specific number ranges per metric across the three scenarios, so I need to pick the most critical metrics and establish what \"low\" and \"high\" look like for each one. I'm focusing on gamma significance across benchmarks with S1 signal, absolute MAE from the calibrated model, percentage improvement over raw MAE, 95% coverage as a fraction, Kendall tau for ranking correlation, how many of the non-S1 signals recover at 1.5B scale, and whether the theoretical thresholds align with what we actually observe empirically.\n\nFor the ranges themselves, I'm aiming for consistency where the best tier shows the strongest signal, the median tier shows moderate or mixed results, and the worst tier shows degradation. For instance, gamma significance might range from 2-3 benchmarks in the best case down to 0-1 in the worst, while MAE improvement could swing from a solid 0.5-3 percentage points better than raw in the best scenario to 1-5 points worse in the worst. Now I'm thinking through what each tier actually means in practice — the best case is when we hit 16+ models with multi-seed pooling and larger data, the simulation sweep works cleanly, and we see real improvements in scaling laws plus strong calibration effects that validate our theoretical thresholds. The median scenario gets us to similar model counts but with some instability, partial improvements, and weaker convergence signals, though the core finding about signal characterization still holds. The worst case is where IRT fundamentally can't outperform simpler baselines no matter how many models we add, convergence fails entirely, and we're left with poor coverage across the board.\n\nNow I'm ready to write up the final YAML summary.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":27133,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":27133},"output_tokens":23,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ad25a373-2e3a-4606-a857-ab808477fb05","uuid":"a95540cb-bc92-44e2-b35a-d24c022f4625"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01NmAksYX7xPWBrWMbTtFL9x","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntiers:\n  best:\n    meaning: \"Multi-seed pooling到N≥16后γ在多benchmark显著，IRT在高污染条件下校准优于raw，signal characterization + identifiability analysis全线成立\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"N_models≥16（multi-seed pooling + 3B v2数据）后MMLU IRT收敛（rhat<1.01, ESS≥400）\"\n      - \"S2(Min-K++)或S4(Self-Critique)在1.5B/3B至少一个恢复到AUC≥0.65，RRF不退化为S1-only\"\n      - \"exp-004 sim sweep v5完成，Fisher Info理论阈值（AUC≥0.70）与实证吻合\"\n      - \"per-benchmark γ_k异质性模式跨seed稳定（ARC负/MMLU正/GSM8K≈0），cross-seed r≥0.85\"\n      - \"Coverage随N增长有实质改善（从当前0.18-0.63升至≥0.50）\"\n    number_ranges:\n      gamma_significant_benchmarks_s1: [2, 3]  # ARC+MMLU confirmed, GSM8K correctly null\n      mae_contaminated_conditions_pp: [2.0, 5.0]  # absolute MAE in pp, lower=better\n      mae_improvement_over_raw_pp: [0.5, 2.5]  # positive=IRT better, on d025/d100 conditions\n      coverage_95: [0.50, 0.80]\n      kendall_tau: [0.70, 0.90]\n      effective_signals_1p5B: [2, 3]  # out of 3\n      irt_convergence_rate: [0.80, 1.00]  # fraction of benchmark fits converging\n      gamma_arc: [-3.0, -0.5]\n      gamma_mmlu: [0.3, 2.0]\n      gamma_gsm8k: [-0.3, 0.3]\n    narrative_direction: >\n      D038 framing完全成立。三条claim均可保持原始强度：C1校准在γ显著的benchmark上\n      确实优于raw（ARC/MMLU），GSM8K作为negative control正确识别为无需校准；C2信号\n      characterization展示scale-dependent行为（S1 robust, S2/S4 scale-sensitive），\n      Min-K++方向反转是社区级cautionary finding；C3 Fisher Info identifiability bounds\n      与实证AUC阈值一致。Coverage虽低于95%名义值但有实质改善，在Limitations中讨论\n      local independence violation作为已知IRT limitation。论文lead with γ_k异质性\n      作为benchmark property的novel empirical finding，calibration improvement作为\n      practical demonstration。\n\n  median:\n    meaning: \"γ仅在ARC稳定显著，MMLU边际或不收敛，校准MAE与raw持平，paper重心从calibration转向characterization+signal findings\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"N_models达到12-16但MMLU IRT收敛仍不稳定（rhat 1.01-1.05）\"\n      - \"S2/S4在1.5B/3B未实质改善（AUC<0.60），RRF实质退化为S1-only\"\n      - \"exp-004 sim sweep部分完成，Fisher Info推导正确但实证验证受限于failed fits\"\n      - \"ARC γ显著且方向一致（负），GSM8K正确null，MMLU γ方向不稳定或CI宽\"\n      - \"Coverage改善有限，仍远低于名义值\"\n    number_ranges:\n      gamma_significant_benchmarks_s1: [1, 2]  # ARC confirmed, MMLU marginal\n      mae_contaminated_conditions_pp: [3.0, 8.0]\n      mae_improvement_over_raw_pp: [-1.5, 0.5]  # roughly comparable to raw\n      coverage_95: [0.25, 0.55]\n      kendall_tau: [0.55, 0.75]\n      effective_signals_1p5B: [1, 2]  # S1 only, maybe S4 marginal\n      irt_convergence_rate: [0.50, 0.80]\n      gamma_arc: [-3.0, -0.4]\n      gamma_mmlu: [-1.0, 1.5]  # wide, possibly non-significant\n      gamma_gsm8k: [-0.5, 0.5]\n    narrative_direction: >\n      C1降级：不claim \"calibration improvement\"，改为\"contamination-aware modeling\n      reveals benchmark-dependent effects\"——γ本身是finding而非校准手段。C2成为论文\n      主力贡献：Min-K++ directional reversal（Cohen's d=-1.98）、S1-only有效性、\n      RRF在signal degradation下的graceful degradation行为。C3保持但scope缩小为\n      theoretical contribution（Fisher Info推导）+有限实证验证。Coverage gap在\n      Discussion中作为open problem深入分析（local independence + small N），引用\n      SBC诊断结果。论文标题可能调整为侧重\"characterizing\"而非\"calibrating\"。\n      仍投EMNLP——signal findings + negative gamma + identifiability analysis三个\n      independent contributions足够。补充实验优先级：multi-family validation(exp-014)\n      用于rebuttal strengthening。\n\n  worst:\n    meaning: \"IRT校准在所有条件下不优于raw baseline，γ仅oracle条件显著，real-signal pipeline无实际校准价值，需完全reframe为negative-results + empirical characterization paper\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"MMLU IRT始终不收敛，ARC γ用real signal后CI包含零或效果被baseline regression匹配\"\n      - \"S2/S4在所有scale均无效（AUC≤0.55），multi-scale claim被迫放弃\"\n      - \"exp-004 sim sweep失败或结果显示realistic条件下（AUC<0.80, N<30）γ不可识别\"\n      - \"Coverage即使N=16仍<30%，PPP极端值（0.0或1.0）表明系统性模型误设定\"\n      - \"Additive γ结构不足以捕捉item-level heterogeneity，4PL+γ模型本身是瓶颈\"\n    number_ranges:\n      gamma_significant_benchmarks_s1: [0, 1]  # maybe none with real signals\n      mae_contaminated_conditions_pp: [5.0, 12.0]\n      mae_improvement_over_raw_pp: [-8.0, -1.0]  # consistently worse than raw\n      coverage_95: [0.10, 0.30]\n      kendall_tau: [0.30, 0.60]\n      effective_signals_1p5B: [1, 1]  # S1 only at all scales\n      irt_convergence_rate: [0.20, 0.50]\n      gamma_arc: [-4.0, 0.5]  # wide, unstable\n      gamma_mmlu: [-5.0, 5.0]  # non-convergent, meaningless\n      gamma_gsm8k: [-1.0, 1.0]\n    narrative_direction: >\n      完全reframe为\"Why Statistical Calibration of Contaminated Benchmarks Fails —\n      and What We Learn from the Attempt\"。三条新contributions替代原始claims：\n      (1) Negative result——在controlled SFT injection下，IRT+contamination covariate\n      不优于trivial baselines，根因分析指向local independence violation + additive γ\n      结构不足 + small N regime，为后续工作划定failure modes；\n      (2) Benchmark-dependent contamination effects的实证发现——ARC灾难性遗忘（-17pp）\n      vs MMLU轻微inflation，这一现象独立于IRT框架成立，仅需raw evaluation data即可\n      展示，是最robust的contribution；\n      (3) Min-K++ directional reversal + signal effectiveness mapping——即使IRT不work，\n      signal characterization对社区仍有直接价值，特别是reversal作为MIA方法在LLM\n      contamination detection中的fundamental limitation。\n      Fisher Info分析作为theoretical contribution保留，但结论变为\"identifiability\n      requires conditions rarely met in practice\"。仍投EMNLP——negative results +\n      special theme alignment + 两个独立empirical findings足够。标题改为characterization/\n      analysis framing，不提calibration。\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":27133,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":27133},"output_tokens":23,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ad25a373-2e3a-4606-a857-ab808477fb05","uuid":"205ca3a1-0475-4f0f-afaa-b415b1d9d5ed"}
